  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Is coral an animal or a plant?",
      "a": "Coral is an animal, related to jellyfish and sea anemones. But reef-building corals have tiny algae living inside them that photosynthesise like plants, which is why coral can look plant-like.",
      "asks": [
        "Is coral alive?",
        "What is coral made of?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do algae help corals?",
      "a": "The algae living in coral tissue use sunlight to make sugars and pass most of them to the coral, giving it much of its food. They also help it build its skeleton faster and give coral its colours.",
      "asks": [
        "What are zooxanthellae?",
        "Why do corals need sunlight?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How fast do coral reefs grow?",
      "a": "Slowly. Boulder-shaped corals grow about 0.3 to 2 centimetres a year, while branching corals can grow 10 centimetres or more. A big reef takes thousands of years to build.",
      "asks": [
        "How old are coral reefs?",
        "How long does it take a reef to form?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is coral bleaching?",
      "a": "When the sea gets too hot for too long, stressed corals push out their algae and turn white, showing their skeleton. Without the algae they starve, and if the heat lasts they can die.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does coral turn white?",
        "What kills coral reefs?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What do corals eat?",
      "a": "Most of their food comes from sugars made by their algae. At night many polyps also stretch out tentacles armed with stinging cells to catch tiny plankton drifting by.",
      "asks": [
        "Do corals catch food?",
        "How do corals feed at night?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Reef-building corals get much of their food from algae called zooxanthellae living in their tissues.",
    "Boulder corals typically grow about 0.3 to 2 centimetres a year, while branching corals can grow 10 centimetres or more."
